<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Go to the Window.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Identify objects on the pages of picture books, using simple words.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can identify objects in picture books, using simple words (e.g., 'desk').
            Can ask for help identifying objects by asking questions (e.g., 'What is this?').
            Can confirm object names by repeating or responding to prompts (e.g., 'Is this a desk?').
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using prepositions and classroom objects. "Simon says: put your hand on your head!" "Simon says: go to the window!" "Simon says: put the book under the chair!" This activates physical response and location vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture card of an object (e.g., a pencil) and places it in different locations on a mini-desk. The teacher points and says, "Where is the pencil? It is *on* the desk. Where is the pencil? It is *under* the desk." Teacher emphasizes /w/ in 'where' and /b/ in 'book' with exaggerated mouth movements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks, "Is the book *on* the table or *under* the table?" (pointing to correct location). The teacher then asks, "Show me *in front of* the box." Students point or demonstrate. "Are there *two* books or *one* book?" (showing two books). Teacher also flashes letters K, L, M, N, O for quick recognition.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book featuring classroom objects (e.g., 'My Classroom Adventures') with simple sentences describing locations like: "The red book is on the large table. The yellow pencil is in the blue box. The small ruler is next to the notebook." or flashcards of various objects (chair, desk, bag, box, glue, marker, ruler, book, pencil).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions (e.g., 'The book is on the desk.'). Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions (e.g., 'Go to the window.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the picture book aloud, pointing to each object and its location. "Look! The book is *on* the table." Students point to the corresponding objects in the book.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks simple gist questions: "Is the pencil *in* the bag or *on* the bag?" (Students respond by pointing or saying "in" or "on").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher gives commands: "Point to the *red* book." "Show me the *blue* chair." "Go to the window."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students repeat simple phrases chorally: "The book is on the desk."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying objects, asking 'What is this?', asking 'Where is...?', and responding with prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to, behind, in front of, between, near) using target vocabulary (chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil, poster, ruler, glue, marker, folder, card, shelf, chart, pen, chalk, key, notebook) and colors (yellow, red, blue, orange, green, purple).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Point and Say":</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of objects and prepositions. The teacher places an object (e.g., a toy book) in a location (e.g., on a toy desk) and asks, "Where is the book?" Students point to the correct preposition flashcard and repeat, "It is *on* the desk." The teacher guides them through 'in', 'under', 'next to', etc.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Find and Share":</b> Students are given a small picture sheet with 3-4 objects placed in different locations (e.g., a red apple *in* a green box, a yellow ruler *under* a blue chair). In pairs, one student points to an object and asks, "Where is the [object]?" The other student responds, "It is [preposition] the [object]." The teacher circulates, checking for correct preposition use and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Ask for Help, Give Help":</b> Students work in pairs with a set of small classroom object flashcards. One student secretly places a flashcard in, on, or under a small item on their desk (e.g., a marker under a box). Their partner asks, "What is this?" and "Where is it, please?" The first student reveals the card, identifies it, and states its location, mediating the information: "This is a *red* marker. It is *under* the *blue* box." (Emphasizing polite request and giving information about locations).</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Preposition Scavenger Hunt - Show and Tell" (21st Century Skills Project). Students work in small groups of 3-4. The teacher gives each group a checklist of 3-4 classroom objects to find (e.g., 'a yellow pencil', 'a blue book', 'a small box').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a list of items. They must find these items within a designated area of the classroom.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When a group finds an item, one student points to it and asks, "Where is the [item]?" Another student (or the group) responds, mediating the information: "It is *on* the shelf!" or "It is *under* the table!" (They are conveying the location of the found object to their group).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once they have found all items on their list, they return to their designated spot.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then "shows and tells" 1-2 items to the class. A student from the group holds up an item or points to it in its found location and says, for example, "We found a *yellow* pencil. It was *in* the *big* box." (Mediation: presenting information about the location). Another student from the group can ask, "Is it *in* the box?" for confirmation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in describing locations using prepositions, collaborative interaction within the group, and clear communication of findings to the class.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Show and Tell" part of the Scavenger Hunt, focusing on individual student contributions to describing locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, as part of their group's presentation, identifies and describes the location of at least one item found during the "Preposition Scavenger Hunt." For instance, a student says, "I found a *blue* book. It is *on* the *small* chair." (Demonstrating mediation of location information).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Identifies objects and colors correctly (e.g., 'blue book').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Prepositions:</b> Correctly uses at least one preposition of place (e.g., 'on', 'under', 'in', 'next to').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Understandable pronunciation of key words and prepositions (e.g., /w/ in 'where', /b/ in 'book').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Participation:</b> Clearly conveys information about the object's location, actively participates in the group's presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Asking/Confirming:</b> Can ask "What is this?" or confirm "Is this a desk?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "happy face" if they can tell where an object is (e.g., "It's on the table!") and a "thinking face" if they need more practice. The teacher also asks: "Can you help your friend find the yellow book?" (Thumbs up/down).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you use today to tell your friends where things are?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like finding things in the classroom? What was your favorite part?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students were highly engaged in the scavenger hunt; the use of realia helped solidify understanding of prepositions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., Provide more visual cues for 'between' and 'behind'; integrate more movement-based activities for kinesthetic learners; simplify the instructions for the scavenger hunt slightly more for some groups).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Where Is It?" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, reinforcing vocabulary and prepositions in a new context, perhaps focusing on "My House" or "My Toys" to continue practicing location descriptions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>